KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ size is 2 after erase_if removes one element: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*invariant violation.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: std::erase_if over a populated std::vector is currently a no-op, so
the size assertion fails (the vector keeps all three elements).

The crash this call used to trigger (a convert_function return-type
exception-safety bug) is fixed and separately regression-tested by
cpp20_erase_if_return_type, so this test no longer aborts CBMC -- it now runs to
a clean assertion FAILURE.

Remaining functional gap: std::erase_if's body does
`__cont.erase(__niter_wrap(__cont.begin(), __removed), __cont.end())`, i.e.
vector::erase(const_iterator, const_iterator) called with `iterator` arguments.
That requires the iterator -> const_iterator user-defined conversion (via
__normal_iterator's converting constructor template, whose parameter is the
class-template-id __normal_iterator<_Iter, _Container>).  During the member-call
overload resolution for erase() the erase candidate is rejected before that
user-defined conversion is applied, so the call is dropped and erase_if is a
no-op (size stays 3).

The converting constructor itself works for direct initialization
(`const_iterator c = it;`), so the gap is specifically in applying this
class-template-id converting-constructor conversion during member-call overload
resolution.  Note that a *direct* `v.erase(v.begin(), v.end())` call hits the
same unresolved overload and silently drops the call together with the rest of
its full-expression, so such uses only appear to pass *vacuously* (the
assertions after them are dropped and never checked).  A minimal header-free
reproducer is cpp20_member_overload_converting_ctor.

Reclassify CORE once the iterator -> const_iterator user-defined conversion is
applied during vector::erase overload resolution so the populated vector shrinks
and size() == 2 holds.
